Lunaris — dwm
=============
The window manager of Lunaris (https://lunaris.shahinabdi.com), a patched dwm 6.8.
Install the whole desktop with https://github.com/shahinabdi/lunaris (install.sh).

dwm - dynamic window manager
============================
dwm is an extremely fast, small, and dynamic window manager for X.


Requirements
------------
In order to build dwm you need the Xlib header files. The included
.xinitrc also starts picom with VSync to prevent screen tearing, so
picom must be installed when using this startup file.


Installation
------------
Edit config.mk to match your local setup (dwm is installed into
the /usr/local namespace by default).

Afterwards enter the following command to build and install dwm (if
necessary as root):

    make clean install


Running dwm
-----------
Add the following line to your .xinitrc to start dwm using startx:

    exec dwm

In order to connect dwm to a specific display, make sure that
the DISPLAY environment variable is set correctly, e.g.:

    DISPLAY=foo.bar:1 exec dwm

(This will start dwm on display :1 of the host foo.bar.)

In order to display status info in the bar, you can do something
like this in your .xinitrc:

    while xsetroot -name "`date` `uptime | sed 's/.*,//'`"
    do
    	sleep 1
    done &
    exec dwm


Configuration
-------------
The configuration of dwm is done by creating a custom config.h
and (re)compiling the source code.


Local setup (Lunaris)
---------------------
Patches: statuscmd (hand-ported to 6.8) — clickable status blocks.
Status bar: scripts/dwm-status (started from .xinitrc), clicks go to
scripts/dwm-statusclick, actions in scripts/dwm-ctl. Scripts are
symlinked into ~/.local/bin.

Bar blocks:   keyboard | network | volume | brightness | battery | date
  keyboard    click: reset layouts (first = fr / us) (also Mod+Shift+k)
  network     left: wifi menu (rofi), right: nmtui (also Mod+n)
  volume      left: mute, right: pavucontrol, scroll: volume
  brightness  scroll: brightness
  battery     click: details
  date        left: calendar
Media keys (volume/mute/mic/brightness) work out of the box.
Mod+Shift+h (or `dwm-help` in a terminal) shows all keybindings — scripts/dwm-help.

Keyboard layout is set system-wide with `sudo localectl set-x11-keymap fr,ir`
(/etc/X11/xorg.conf.d/00-keyboard.conf) so reconnected keyboards keep it.


Keyboard profiles (AZERTY default, QWERTY option)
-------------------------------------------------
dwm matches the key's unshifted symbol, so the workspace and screen keys
depend on the keyboard. They live in keyboard.h and are picked at build time:

    make                     AZERTY (default): Mod + & é " ' ( - è _ ç à
                             = workspaces 1-10, Mod + ; / , = next / previous screen
    make KEYBOARD=qwerty     QWERTY: Mod + 1 ... 9 0 = workspaces 1-10,
                             Mod + . / , = next / previous screen

Every other binding is the same on both. The profile is also saved in
~/.config/dwm-btw.conf (KEYBOARD=azerty|qwerty, LAYOUTS=fr,ir / us,ir), which
dwm-dev build (Rebuild in the sidebar), dwm-help, dwm-ctl kb-reset and
.xinitrc read. No file = AZERTY with fr,ir.

    dwm-keyboard                       show the profile
    dwm-keyboard set qwerty [us,ir]    save, set layouts, rebuild + restart dwm
    dwm-keyboard set azerty            back to AZERTY

Another layout (German QWERTZ, Dvorak...): edit the QWERTY block in
keyboard.h and build with KEYBOARD=qwerty. Change the keysyms (see /usr/include/X11/keysymdef.h, or run
`xev` and press the key to see its name).

Workspace icons and app rules
-----------------------------
tags.h / rules.h are the defaults. Change them without rebuilding from the sidebar
(Super+A -> gear -> Workspaces) or with dwm-workspaces; they are saved in
~/.config/dwm-btw/workspaces.conf, which dwm reads when it starts or restarts in place:

    icon 8 <text or Nerd Font glyph>          (https://www.nerdfonts.com/cheat-sheet)
    rule <class>|<instance>|<title>|<workspace, 0 = any>|<floating 0/1>|<follow 0/1>

Delete the file (or "Reset to defaults") to go back to tags.h / rules.h.

